    m4rref: `<p>La forma a scala ridotta per righe dell'aumentata \u00e8:</p>`,
    m4badRow: (r) => `<p>La riga \\(${r}\\) della RREF \u00e8 del tipo \\((0\\;\\cdots\\;0\\,|\\,1)\\): si legge \\(0=1\\), un'equazione senza soluzioni. Il pivot cade nella colonna dei termini noti, esattamente il criterio di Rouch\u00e9-Capelli: il sistema \u00e8 <b>impossibile</b>.</p>`,
    m4vars: (piv, free) => `<p>Le variabili <b>pivot</b> sono \\(${piv}\\); le variabili <b>libere</b> sono \\(${free}\\). Da ogni riga non nulla si ricava una variabile pivot in funzione del termine noto e delle variabili libere:</p>`,
    m4unique: `<p>Non ci sono variabili libere: le equazioni della RREF danno direttamente il valore di ciascuna incognita. L'unica soluzione \u00e8:</p>`,
    m4part: `<p>Ponendo tutte le variabili libere a \\(0\\) si ottiene la <b>soluzione particolare</b>:</p>`,
    m4hom: (free) => `<p>Per la parte omogenea, si assegna a turno il valore \\(1\\) a una variabile libera e \\(0\\) alle altre (\\(${free}\\)): ogni assegnazione d\u00e0 un vettore del nucleo, e insieme formano una base:</p>`,

    m5structIntro: `<p>La soluzione generale \u00e8 <b>soluzione particolare pi\u00f9 nucleo</b>: \\(\\operatorname{Sol}=\\mathbf{x}_p+\\operatorname{Ker}f\\), un sottospazio affine. In <b>forma parametrica</b>, usando come parametri le variabili libere stesse:</p>`,
    m5checkXpEq: (ok) => ok
      ? `<p>Verifica incrociata: la soluzione trovata a occhio coincide con quella di Gauss-Jordan. \u2713</p>`
      : `<p>Attenzione: la soluzione trovata a occhio non coincide con quella di Gauss-Jordan \u2014 va ricontrollata.</p>`,
    m5checkXp: (ok) => ok
      ? `<p>Verifica incrociata: la soluzione particolare trovata a occhio pu\u00f2 differire da quella di Gauss-Jordan (una qualunque soluzione va bene come \\(\\mathbf{x}_p\\)). Controlliamo che la differenza fra le due appartenga al nucleo \u2014 aggiungendola alla base del nucleo il rango non cresce \u2014 ed \u00e8 cos\u00ec: entrambe descrivono lo stesso insieme di soluzioni. \u2713</p>`
      : `<p>Attenzione: la differenza fra la soluzione a occhio e quella di Gauss-Jordan non appartiene al nucleo \u2014 una delle due va ricontrollata.</p>`,
    m5checkKer: `<p>Le basi del nucleo (scorciatoie e Gauss-Jordan) possono non coincidere vettore per vettore: possono differire per segno, multiplo o combinazione lineare. Verifichiamo che generino lo <b>stesso sottospazio</b> tramite i ranghi:</p>`,
    m5tex: (ra, rb, ru) => `\\operatorname{rank}(\\text{scorciatoie})=${ra},\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\text{standard})=${rb},\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\text{unione})=${ru}`,
    m5same: `<p>I tre ranghi coincidono: le scorciatoie e la base standard generano esattamente lo stesso nucleo. \u2713</p>`,
    m5conclIncomp: `<p><b>Conclusione.</b> Il sistema \u00e8 impossibile:</p>`,
    m5conclIncompTex: `\\operatorname{Sol}=\\varnothing`,
    m5conclUnique: `<p><b>Conclusione.</b> Il sistema ha un'unica soluzione:</p>`,
    m5concl: (d) => `<p><b>Conclusione.</b> Il sistema ha \\(\\infty^{${d}}\\) soluzioni, descritte dalla forma parametrica:</p>`,

    // parametrico
    pGenericCompat: (ra, rg, d) => `<p>La matrice contiene un parametro. Riducendo l'aumentata con Gauss-Jordan dividiamo per i pivot, assumendo implicitamente che siano diversi da zero; sotto queste assunzioni \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) e \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\): per valori <b>generici</b> del parametro il sistema \u00e8 <b>compatibile</b> con \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'soluzione (unica)' : 'soluzioni'}. I pivot espliciti, e quindi le condizioni di genericit\u00e0, li leggeremo dalla RREF.</p>`,
    pGenericIncompat: (ra, rg) => `<p>La matrice contiene un parametro. Riducendo l'aumentata con Gauss-Jordan dividiamo per i pivot, assumendo implicitamente che siano diversi da zero; sotto queste assunzioni \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) ma \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\): per valori <b>generici</b> del parametro il sistema \u00e8 <b>impossibile</b>. Solo i valori critici (dove qualche pivot si annulla) potranno eventualmente renderlo compatibile.</p>`,
    pAllCompat: (ra, rg, d) => `<p>La matrice contiene un parametro, ma la riduzione non ha mai richiesto di dividere per un'espressione nel parametro (\u00e8 sempre esistito un pivot costante): \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) e \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\) valgono per <b>ogni</b> valore del parametro. Il sistema \u00e8 <b>compatibile</b> con \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'soluzione (unica)' : 'soluzioni'} per ogni valore, senza casi da distinguere.</p>`,
    pAllIncompat: (ra, rg) => `<p>La matrice contiene un parametro, ma la riduzione non ha mai richiesto di dividere per un'espressione nel parametro (\u00e8 sempre esistito un pivot costante): \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) e \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\) valgono per <b>ogni</b> valore del parametro. Il sistema \u00e8 <b>impossibile</b> per ogni valore, senza casi da distinguere.</p>`,
    pAssume: (list) => `<p>Le condizioni di genericit\u00e0 sono proprio i pivot per cui abbiamo diviso, che ritrovi come denominatori nella RREF qui sopra. Esplicitamente: \\(${list.map((s) => s + '\\neq 0').join(',\\quad ')}\\). I valori del parametro che le violano vanno esaminati a parte, perch\u00e9 l\u00ec rango e compatibilit\u00e0 possono cambiare.</p>`,
    pNoAssume: `<p>Nessun pivot simbolico: non abbiamo mai diviso per espressioni nel parametro, quindi la RREF e le conclusioni valgono per <b>ogni</b> valore del parametro.</p>`,
    pGenericSol: `<p>Sotto le assunzioni di genericit\u00e0, la lettura della RREF d\u00e0 la soluzione del caso generico:</p>`,
    pAllSol: `<p>La lettura della RREF d\u00e0 la soluzione, valida per ogni valore del parametro:</p>`,
    pCaseBecomesIncomp: (param, val) => `<p>Per \\(${param}=${val}\\) il sistema <b>diventa impossibile</b>: il rango di \\(A\\) cala ma quello dell'aumentata no. Sostituiamo il valore e verifichiamolo.</p>`,
    pCaseBecomesCompat: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>Per \\(${param}=${val}\\) il sistema <b>diventa compatibile</b>: i due ranghi si allineano a \\(${k}\\), con \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'soluzione (unica)' : 'soluzioni'}. Sostituiamo il valore e risolviamo.</p>`,
    pCaseDrop: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>Per \\(${param}=${val}\\) il sistema resta compatibile ma il rango cala a \\(${k}\\): le soluzioni diventano \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\). Sostituiamo il valore nella matrice e ricalcoliamo tutto da capo.</p>`,
    pCaseStillIncomp: (param, val) => `<p>Per \\(${param}=${val}\\) il sistema resta <b>impossibile</b> (il valore era solo un candidato). Sostituiamo per verificarlo.</p>`,
    pCaseNoChange: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>Per \\(${param}=${val}\\) non cambia nulla: il rango resta \\(${k}\\) e il sistema resta compatibile con \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'soluzione' : 'soluzioni'} (il valore era solo un candidato, perch\u00e9 un pivot intermedio si annulla). Sostituiamo per verificarlo e per ottenere una soluzione valida qui (le formule generiche possono avere denominatori che si annullano).</p>`,
    pCaseSubst: (param, val) => `<p>Sostituiamo \\(${param}=${val}\\) nelle formule della matrice aumentata (valore incorniciato) e svolgiamo i conti:</p>`,
    pCaseRref: `<p>e la sua forma a scala ridotta per righe (RREF) \u00e8:</p>`,
    pCaseSolUnique: `<p>Non ci sono variabili libere: l'unica soluzione in questo caso \u00e8:</p>`,
    pCaseSolParam: (d) => `<p>Da cui, in questo caso, \\(\\infty^{${d}}\\) soluzioni in forma parametrica:</p>`,
    pNonRational: (tex, approx, degree, realCount) => {
      let s = `<p>I restanti valori critici sono le radici dell'equazione \\(${tex}\\). Per questo polinomio sono <b>irrazionali</b>: non si possono scrivere come frazioni esatte, e chiuderle in forma esatta richiederebbe l'aritmetica sui numeri algebrici, fuori dalla portata di questo strumento, che lavora su numeri razionali. In corrispondenza di ciascuna, rango e compatibilit\u00e0 vanno ricontrollati.</p>`;
      if (approx && approx.length) {
        const more = degree > realCount ? ` (le altre \\(${degree - realCount}\\) radici non sono reali)` : '';
        s += `<p>Per orientarti, ${realCount === 1 ? 'la radice reale vale' : 'le radici reali valgono'} circa \\(${approx.join(',\\quad ')}\\)${more}. Per studiare il sistema in uno di questi valori, sostituiscilo (anche in forma approssimata) e risolvi il sistema numerico, ad esempio con il risolutore di Gauss-Jordan.</p>`;
      } else {
        s += `<p>Questo polinomio non ha radici reali, quindi il caso non si presenta per valori reali del parametro.</p>`;
      }
      return s;
    },
    pDeclared: (tex) => `<p>La condizione \\(${tex}\\) coinvolge pi\u00f9 di un parametro: non la chiudo automaticamente, perch\u00e9 enumerarne tutti i sottocasi \u00e8 un problema molto pi\u00f9 ampio. Va analizzata a parte sostituendola nella matrice.</p>`,
    pRecursion: (param, val, resid) => `<p>Sostituendo \\(${param}=${val}\\), la matrice resta in funzione ${resid.length ? `del parametro \\(${resid.join(',')}\\)` : 'di nessun altro parametro'}; si richiude il ragionamento generico su ci\u00f2 che rimane.</p>`,
    quickGenericLabel: `<p><b>Caso generico:</b></p>`,
    quickCaseLabel: (param, val) => `<p><b>Caso \\(${param}=${val}\\):</b></p>`,
  };

  const en = {
    title: 'Solving a linear system',
    m1: 'Compatibility and number of solutions (Rouch\u00e9-Capelli)',
    m2: 'Searching for shortcuts',
    m3: 'Evaluating the shortcuts',
    m4: 'Solving with Gauss-Jordan',
    m5: 'Parametric form and conclusion',
    mCases: 'Case analysis',

    intro: (m, n) => `<p>The system has \\(${m}\\) ${m === 1 ? 'equation' : 'equations'} and \\(${n}\\) ${n === 1 ? 'unknown' : 'unknowns'}: in matrix form it reads \\(A \\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\\), with \\(A\\) of size \\(${m}\\times ${n}\\).</p>`,
    augLabel: `<p>All the information of the system sits in the <b>augmented matrix</b> \\([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}]\\), which we will work on:</p>`,

    m1body: `<p>By the <b>Rouch\u00e9-Capelli theorem</b> the system is consistent if and only if \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])\\). Reducing the augmented matrix we count pivots: those falling in the columns of \\(A\\) give the rank of \\(A\\), the total gives the rank of the augmented matrix. Here:</p>`,
    m1tex: (ra, rg) => `\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra},\\qquad \\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}`,
    m1incompat: `<p>The two ranks are <b>different</b>: \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) does not belong to the column space of \\(A\\), so the system is <b>inconsistent</b>: \\(\\operatorname{Sol}=\\varnothing\\). We will confirm this along the standard route, where the inconsistency shows up as a row of the form \\((0\\;\\cdots\\;0\\,|\\,1)\\), i.e. the equation \\(0=1\\).</p>`,
    m1compat: `<p>The two ranks agree: the system is <b>consistent</b>. The number of free parameters of the general solution is:</p>`,
    m1dimTex: (n, k, d) => `d \\;=\\; n-\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\;=\\;${n}-${k}\\;=\\;${d}`,
    m1unique: `<p>Since \\(d=0\\), the solution is <b>unique</b>. We will determine it by Gauss-Jordan.</p>`,
    m1inf: (d) => `<p>The system has \\(\\infty^{${d}}\\) solutions. This number is our compass: the final parametric form must have exactly \\(${d}\\) free ${d === 1 ? 'parameter' : 'parameters'} (one particular solution plus \\(${d}\\) kernel ${d === 1 ? 'vector' : 'vectors'}).</p>`,

    m2intro: `<p>Before the standard route, three checks one does by eye: (1) <b>evident inconsistencies</b> \u2014 rows of the form \\(0=c\\) with \\(c\\neq 0\\), or equations proportional on the left but not on the right; (2) a visible <b>particular solution</b> \u2014 when \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is zero, or coincides with a column of \\(A\\), a multiple of one, or a simple combination of columns (if \\(\\sum_j \\lambda_j\\,\\mathbf{c}_j=\\mathbf{b}\\), then \\(\\mathbf{x}\\) with \\(x_j=\\lambda_j\\) solves the system); (3) <b>relations among the columns</b> of \\(A\\), which give the kernel vectors, i.e. the homogeneous part of the general solution.</p>`,
    relZeroRow: (i, c) => `<p>Row \\(${i}\\) of \\(A\\) is entirely zero, but the corresponding right-hand side is \\(${c}\\neq 0\\): the equation reads \\(0=${c}\\), which has no solutions.</p>`,
    relPropRows: (i, j, lam) => `<p>Row \\(${j}\\) of \\(A\\) is \\(\\left(${lam}\\right)\\) times row \\(${i}\\), but the right-hand sides do not follow the same proportion: subtracting \\(\\left(${lam}\\right)\\) times equation \\(${i}\\) from equation \\(${j}\\) leaves \\(0=c\\) with \\(c\\neq0\\). The two equations contradict each other.</p>`,
    m2incompFound: `<p>This alone shows <b>by eye</b> that the system is inconsistent: \\(\\operatorname{Sol}=\\varnothing\\). There is no point searching for particular solutions or kernel vectors; the standard route below will serve as a rigorous check.</p>`,
    m2noIncomp: `<p>No evident inconsistency by eye.</p>`,
    relBZero: `<p>The right-hand side is \\(\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}\\): the system is homogeneous, and \\(\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\\) is an immediate (particular) solution.</p>`,
    relBCol: (j) => `<p>The right-hand side coincides with column \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\): since \\(A\\,\\mathbf{e}_{${j}}=\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}=\\mathbf{b}\\), the vector \\(\\mathbf{e}_{${j}}\\) (with \\(1\\) in position \\(${j}\\), \\(0\\) elsewhere) is a particular solution.</p>`,
    relBMult: (j, lam) => `<p>The right-hand side is a multiple of column \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\): \\(\\mathbf{b}=\\left(${lam}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\), so the vector with \\(${lam}\\) in position \\(${j}\\) and \\(0\\) elsewhere is a particular solution.</p>`,
    relBCombo: (parts) => `<p>The right-hand side is a simple linear combination of columns of \\(A\\): \\(\\mathbf{b}=${parts.map((p) => `\\left(${p.coeff}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${p.col}}`).join('+')}\\). The coefficients of the combination, placed in the corresponding positions, give a particular solution.</p>`,
    xpLabel: `<p>The particular solution found by eye is:</p>`,
    xpSym: '\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{eye}}',
    m2noPart: `<p>No particular solution is visible by eye (the right-hand side is not a simple combination of the columns).</p>`,
    m2kerIntro: `<p>We turn to the relations among the columns of \\(A\\): each relation is a kernel vector, i.e. a solution of the associated homogeneous system, needed for the \u201cfree\u201d part of the general solution.</p>`,
    relNull: (j) => `<p>Column \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\) is zero. Since \\(A\\,\\mathbf{e}_{${j}}=\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}=\\mathbf{0}\\), the vector \\(\\mathbf{e}_{${j}}\\) belongs to the kernel.</p>`,
    relMult: (j, i, lam) => `<p>Column \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\) is a multiple of the earlier \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${i}}\\): \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}=\\left(${lam}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${i}}\\), i.e. \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}-\\left(${lam}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${i}}=\\mathbf{0}\\). The kernel vector has \\(1\\) in position \\(${j}\\) and \\(${neg(lam)}\\) in position \\(${i}\\).</p>`,
    relCombo: (j, parts) => `<p>Column \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}\\) is a linear combination of previous columns: \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}=${parts.map((p) => `\\left(${p.coeff}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${p.col}}`).join('+')}\\). Rewriting it as \\(\\mathbf{c}_{${j}}-\\big(${parts.map((p) => `\\left(${p.coeff}\\right)\\mathbf{c}_{${p.col}}`).join('+')}\\big)=\\mathbf{0}\\) gives a kernel vector with \\(1\\) in position \\(${j}\\) and the opposite coefficients in the positions involved.</p>`,
    relVecLabel: `<p>The kernel vectors found are:</p>`,
    m2foundKer: (r) => `<p>In all, the shortcuts find \\(${r}\\) kernel ${r === 1 ? 'vector' : 'vectors'}.</p>`,
    m2noKer: `<p>No simple relation among the columns: no shortcut for the homogeneous part.</p>`,
    m2skipKer: `<p>The system is inconsistent, so searching for relations among the columns (which would serve the homogeneous part of the solution) is of no use here: we skip it.</p>`,

    m3incomp: `<p>The shortcuts have already settled the matter: the system is inconsistent. The standard route below is the rigorous confirmation (and shows where the inconsistency emerges in the reduction).</p>`,
    m3sysIncomp: `<p>Rouch\u00e9-Capelli says the system is inconsistent: no shortcut could have found a particular solution. Consistently, none was found. We proceed with the standard route to see where the inconsistency arises.</p>`,
    m3needs: (d) => d === 0
      ? `<p>Compass: since \\(d=0\\), all we need is the unique solution (a \u201cparticular solution\u201d with no free part).</p>`
      : `<p>Compass: to write the general solution we need one <b>particular solution</b> and \\(${d}\\) independent <b>kernel</b> ${d === 1 ? 'vector' : 'vectors'}.</p>`,
    m3partYes: `<p>We already found the particular solution by eye. \u2713</p>`,
    m3partNo: `<p>No particular solution emerged by eye: Gauss-Jordan will provide it.</p>`,
    m3kerEq: (r, d) => `<p>The shortcuts found \\(${r}\\) kernel ${r === 1 ? 'vector' : 'vectors'}, and the kernel must have dimension \\(${d}\\): the homogeneous part is already complete (a <b>candidate basis</b>). The shortcut vectors are independent by construction, each has its \\(1\\) in a different position.</p>`,
    m3kerPart: (r, d) => `<p>The shortcuts found \\(${r}\\) kernel ${r === 1 ? 'vector' : 'vectors'}, but the kernel has dimension \\(${d}\\): \\(${d - r}\\) ${d - r === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} missing. We will complete with Gauss-Jordan.</p>`,
    m3kerNone: (d) => `<p>No kernel shortcut; the kernel has dimension \\(${d}\\): Gauss-Jordan will determine it.</p>`,
    m3go: `<p>In any case, we walk the full <b>standard route</b>: it rigorously confirms what we found and produces the final parametric form.</p>`,

    m4intro: `<p>We reduce the augmented matrix \\([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}]\\) to reduced row echelon form (RREF) by Gauss-Jordan. The <i>reduced</i> form is handy because it lets us read the pivot variables directly in terms of the right-hand side and the free variables. The detailed row operations are those of the Gauss-Jordan solver; here we use its result.</p>`,
    m4rref: `<p>The reduced row echelon form of the augmented matrix is:</p>`,
    m4badRow: (r) => `<p>Row \\(${r}\\) of the RREF has the form \\((0\\;\\cdots\\;0\\,|\\,1)\\): it reads \\(0=1\\), an equation with no solutions. The pivot falls in the right-hand-side column, exactly the Rouch\u00e9-Capelli criterion: the system is <b>inconsistent</b>.</p>`,
    m4vars: (piv, free) => `<p>The <b>pivot</b> variables are \\(${piv}\\); the <b>free</b> variables are \\(${free}\\). Each non-zero row gives a pivot variable in terms of the right-hand side and the free variables:</p>`,
    m4unique: `<p>There are no free variables: the RREF equations give the value of each unknown directly. The unique solution is:</p>`,
    m4part: `<p>Setting all free variables to \\(0\\) yields the <b>particular solution</b>:</p>`,
    m4hom: (free) => `<p>For the homogeneous part, set each free variable to \\(1\\) in turn and the others to \\(0\\) (\\(${free}\\)): each assignment gives a kernel vector, and together they form a basis:</p>`,

    m5structIntro: `<p>The general solution is <b>particular solution plus kernel</b>: \\(\\operatorname{Sol}=\\mathbf{x}_p+\\operatorname{Ker}f\\), an affine subspace. In <b>parametric form</b>, using the free variables themselves as parameters:</p>`,
    m5checkXpEq: (ok) => ok
      ? `<p>Cross-check: the solution found by eye coincides with the Gauss-Jordan one. \u2713</p>`
      : `<p>Warning: the solution found by eye does not coincide with the Gauss-Jordan one \u2014 it should be re-checked.</p>`,
    m5checkXp: (ok) => ok
      ? `<p>Cross-check: the particular solution found by eye may differ from the Gauss-Jordan one (any solution works as \\(\\mathbf{x}_p\\)). We check that their difference belongs to the kernel \u2014 adding it to the kernel basis does not increase the rank \u2014 and indeed it does: both describe the same solution set. \u2713</p>`
      : `<p>Warning: the difference between the by-eye solution and the Gauss-Jordan one does not belong to the kernel \u2014 one of the two should be re-checked.</p>`,
    m5checkKer: `<p>The two kernel bases (shortcuts and Gauss-Jordan) need not match vector by vector: they may differ by sign, scalar multiple or linear combination. We check that they span the <b>same subspace</b> via ranks:</p>`,
    m5tex: (ra, rb, ru) => `\\operatorname{rank}(\\text{shortcuts})=${ra},\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\text{standard})=${rb},\\quad \\operatorname{rank}(\\text{union})=${ru}`,
    m5same: `<p>The three ranks agree: the shortcuts and the standard basis span exactly the same kernel. \u2713</p>`,
    m5conclIncomp: `<p><b>Conclusion.</b> The system is inconsistent:</p>`,
    m5conclIncompTex: `\\operatorname{Sol}=\\varnothing`,
    m5conclUnique: `<p><b>Conclusion.</b> The system has a unique solution:</p>`,
    m5concl: (d) => `<p><b>Conclusion.</b> The system has \\(\\infty^{${d}}\\) solutions, described by the parametric form:</p>`,

    pGenericCompat: (ra, rg, d) => `<p>The matrix contains a parameter. Reducing the augmented matrix with Gauss-Jordan divides by the pivots, implicitly assuming they are non-zero; under these assumptions \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\): for <b>generic</b> parameter values the system is <b>consistent</b> with \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'solution (unique)' : 'solutions'}. The explicit pivots, hence the genericity conditions, we will read from the RREF.</p>`,
    pGenericIncompat: (ra, rg) => `<p>The matrix contains a parameter. Reducing the augmented matrix with Gauss-Jordan divides by the pivots, implicitly assuming they are non-zero; under these assumptions \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) but \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\): for <b>generic</b> parameter values the system is <b>inconsistent</b>. Only the critical values (where some pivot vanishes) may possibly restore consistency.</p>`,
    pAllCompat: (ra, rg, d) => `<p>The matrix contains a parameter, but the reduction never required dividing by an expression in the parameter (a constant pivot was always available): \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\) hold for <b>every</b> value of the parameter. The system is <b>consistent</b> with \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'solution (unique)' : 'solutions'} for every value, with no cases to distinguish.</p>`,
    pAllIncompat: (ra, rg) => `<p>The matrix contains a parameter, but the reduction never required dividing by an expression in the parameter (a constant pivot was always available): \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=${ra}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{rank}([A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}])=${rg}\\) hold for <b>every</b> value of the parameter. The system is <b>inconsistent</b> for every value, with no cases to distinguish.</p>`,
    pAssume: (list) => `<p>The genericity conditions are exactly the pivots we divided by, which you find as denominators in the RREF above. Explicitly: \\(${list.map((s) => s + '\\neq 0').join(',\\quad ')}\\). Parameter values violating them must be examined separately, since rank and consistency may change there.</p>`,
    pNoAssume: `<p>No symbolic pivot: we never divided by expressions in the parameter, so the RREF and the conclusions hold for <b>every</b> value of the parameter.</p>`,
    pGenericSol: `<p>Under the genericity assumptions, reading the RREF gives the generic-case solution:</p>`,
    pAllSol: `<p>Reading the RREF gives the solution, valid for every value of the parameter:</p>`,
    pCaseBecomesIncomp: (param, val) => `<p>For \\(${param}=${val}\\) the system <b>becomes inconsistent</b>: the rank of \\(A\\) drops but the rank of the augmented matrix does not. We substitute the value and verify it.</p>`,
    pCaseBecomesCompat: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>For \\(${param}=${val}\\) the system <b>becomes consistent</b>: the two ranks align at \\(${k}\\), with \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'solution (unique)' : 'solutions'}. We substitute the value and solve.</p>`,
    pCaseDrop: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>For \\(${param}=${val}\\) the system stays consistent but the rank drops to \\(${k}\\): the solutions become \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\). We substitute the value into the matrix and recompute everything from scratch.</p>`,
    pCaseStillIncomp: (param, val) => `<p>For \\(${param}=${val}\\) the system stays <b>inconsistent</b> (the value was only a candidate). We substitute to verify it.</p>`,
    pCaseNoChange: (param, val, k, d) => `<p>For \\(${param}=${val}\\) nothing changes: the rank stays \\(${k}\\) and the system stays consistent with \\(${fmtInf(d)}\\) ${d === 0 ? 'solution' : 'solutions'} (the value was only a candidate, since an intermediate pivot vanishes). We substitute to verify it and to obtain a solution valid here (the generic formulas may have vanishing denominators).</p>`,
    pCaseSubst: (param, val) => `<p>We substitute \\(${param}=${val}\\) into the augmented matrix formulas (boxed value) and carry out the computation:</p>`,
    pCaseRref: `<p>and its reduced row echelon form (RREF) is:</p>`,
    pCaseSolUnique: `<p>There are no free variables: the unique solution in this case is:</p>`,
    pCaseSolParam: (d) => `<p>Hence, in this case, \\(\\infty^{${d}}\\) solutions in parametric form:</p>`,
    pNonRational: (tex, approx, degree, realCount) => {
      let s = `<p>The remaining critical values are the roots of \\(${tex}\\). For this polynomial they are <b>irrational</b>: they cannot be written as exact fractions, and closing them exactly would require algebraic-number arithmetic, beyond the reach of this tool, which works over the rationals. At each of them, rank and consistency must be re-checked.</p>`;
      if (approx && approx.length) {
        const more = degree > realCount ? ` (the other \\(${degree - realCount}\\) roots are not real)` : '';
        s += `<p>For orientation, ${realCount === 1 ? 'the real root is' : 'the real roots are'} about \\(${approx.join(',\\quad ')}\\)${more}. To study the system at one of these values, substitute it (even in approximate form) and solve the resulting numeric system, e.g. with the Gauss-Jordan solver.</p>`;
      } else {
        s += `<p>This polynomial has no real roots, so the case does not occur for real values of the parameter.</p>`;
      }
      return s;
    },
    pDeclared: (tex) => `<p>The condition \\(${tex}\\) involves more than one parameter: it is not closed automatically, since enumerating all its sub-cases is a much larger problem. Analyze it separately by substituting it into the matrix.</p>`,
    pRecursion: (param, val, resid) => `<p>Substituting \\(${param}=${val}\\), the matrix still depends ${resid.length ? `on the parameter \\(${resid.join(',')}\\)` : 'on no further parameter'}; we re-close the generic reasoning on what remains.</p>`,
    quickGenericLabel: `<p><b>Generic case:</b></p>`,
    quickCaseLabel: (param, val) => `<p><b>Case \\(${param}=${val}\\):</b></p>`,
  };

  return lang === 'en' ? en : it;
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- orchestratore
function buildNarration(aCells, bCells, lang = 'it') {
  const t = T(lang);
  const { A, b, aug, field, vars } = buildSystem(aCells, bCells);
  const m = A.length, n = m ? A[0].length : 0;
  const blocks = [];
  const push = (html, tex) => blocks.push(tex !== undefined ? { html, tex } : { html });

  push(`<h2>${t.title}</h2>` + t.intro(m, n),
    `A \\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:\\qquad ${matTex(A)} \\cdot ${xVecTex(n)} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(b)}`);
  push(t.augLabel, `[A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}] \\;=\\; ${augTex(aug, n)}`);

  if (vars.length > 0) return blocks.concat(narrateParametric(A, b, aug, field, vars, n, t));
  return blocks.concat(narrateNumeric(A, b, aug, field, n, t));
}

// versione sintetica: solo sistema + soluzione finale, senza la narrazione dei passaggi
function buildQuickResult(aCells, bCells, lang = 'it') {
  const t = T(lang);
  const { A, b, aug, field, vars } = buildSystem(aCells, bCells);
  const m = A.length, n = m ? A[0].length : 0;
  const blocks = [];
  const push = (html, tex) => blocks.push(tex !== undefined ? { html, tex } : { html });

  push(`<h2>${t.title}</h2>`,
    `A \\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:\\qquad ${matTex(A)} \\cdot ${xVecTex(n)} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(b)}`);

  if (vars.length === 0) {
    const sys = closeSystem(aug, field, n);
    if (sys.incompatible) { push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex); return blocks; }
    if (sys.nullity === 0) push(t.m5conclUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(sys.xp)}`);
    else push(t.m5concl(sys.nullity), paramFormTex(sys.xp, sys.kbasis, sys.freeCols));
    return blocks;
  }

  const a = analyzeParametricSystem(aug, field, n);
  const g = a.generic;
  push(t.quickGenericLabel);
  if (g.incompatible) push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex);
  else if (g.nullity === 0) push(t.m5conclUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(g.xp)}`);
  else push(t.m5concl(g.nullity), paramFormTex(g.xp, g.kbasis, g.freeCols));
  if (g.assumptions.length) push(t.pAssume(g.assumptions.map((x) => condTex(x.element.num))));

  for (const br of a.branches) {
    push(t.quickCaseLabel(br.condition.param, br.condition.value.toLatex()));
    const c = br.closed;
    if (c.incompatible) push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex);
    else if (c.nullity === 0) push(t.m5conclUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(c.xp)}`);
    else push(t.m5concl(c.nullity), paramFormTex(c.xp, c.kbasis, c.freeCols));
  }
  for (const dc of a.declaredConditions) {
    const tex = condTex(dc.poly) + ' = 0';
    if (dc.kind === 'nonrational') push(t.pNonRational(tex, dc.approx, dc.degree, dc.realCount));
    else push(t.pDeclared(tex));
  }
  return blocks;
}

// scorciatoie (comuni a numerico e parametrico): narrazione dei momenti 2 e 3.
// Ritorna anche i dati per i confronti del momento 5.
function narrateShortcuts(A, b, field, d, sysIncompatible, t, push) {
  push(`<h3>${t.m2}</h3>` + t.m2intro);

  // (a) incompatibilità evidenti
  const eyeInc = findEyeIncompatibilities(A, b, field);
  let eyePart = null;
  let colSc = { relations: [], vectors: [], count: 0 };

  if (eyeInc.length > 0) {
    for (const inc of eyeInc) {
      if (inc.kind === 'zeroRow') push(t.relZeroRow(inc.row + 1, inc.rhs.toLatex()));
      else push(t.relPropRows(inc.i + 1, inc.j + 1, inc.lam.toLatex()));
    }
    push(t.m2incompFound);
  } else {
    push(t.m2noIncomp);

    // (b) soluzione particolare a occhio
    eyePart = findEyeParticular(A, b, field);
    if (eyePart) {
      if (eyePart.kind === 'bzero') push(t.relBZero);
      else if (eyePart.kind === 'bcol') push(t.relBCol(eyePart.parts[0].col + 1));
      else if (eyePart.kind === 'bmult') push(t.relBMult(eyePart.parts[0].col + 1, eyePart.parts[0].coeff.toLatex()));
      else push(t.relBCombo(eyePart.parts.map((p) => ({ col: p.col + 1, coeff: p.coeff.toLatex() }))));
      push(t.xpLabel, `${t.xpSym} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(eyePart.xp)}`);
    } else {
      push(t.m2noPart);
    }

    // (c) relazioni fra colonne (parte omogenea) — solo se il sistema può avere soluzioni
    if (sysIncompatible) {
      push(t.m2skipKer);
    } else {
      push(t.m2kerIntro);
      colSc = findColumnShortcuts(A, field);
      if (colSc.count === 0) {
        push(t.m2noKer);
      } else {
        for (const rel of colSc.relations) {
          const j = rel.target + 1;
          if (rel.type === 'null') push(t.relNull(j));
          else if (rel.type === 'multiple') push(t.relMult(j, rel.parts[0].col + 1, rel.parts[0].coeff.toLatex()));
          else push(t.relCombo(j, rel.parts.map((p) => ({ col: p.col + 1, coeff: p.coeff.toLatex() }))));
        }
        push(t.relVecLabel, basisTex(colSc.vectors));
        push(t.m2foundKer(colSc.count));
      }
    }
  }

  // --- Momento 3: valutazione
  push(`<h3>${t.m3}</h3>`);
  if (eyeInc.length > 0) {
    push(t.m3incomp);
  } else if (sysIncompatible) {
    push(t.m3sysIncomp);
  } else {
    push(t.m3needs(d));
    push(eyePart ? t.m3partYes : t.m3partNo);
    if (d > 0) {
      if (colSc.count === 0) push(t.m3kerNone(d));
      else if (colSc.count === d) push(t.m3kerEq(colSc.count, d));
      else push(t.m3kerPart(colSc.count, d));
    }
    push(t.m3go);
  }
  return { eyeInc, eyePart, colSc };
}

// lettura della RREF + soluzione (particolare, nucleo, forma parametrica) di un sistema chiuso.
// labels: { unique, part, hom } per riusare gli stessi testi nel caso generico e nei rami.
function narrateSolutionFromRref(sys, n, t, push, opts = {}) {
  if (sys.nullity === 0) {
    push(opts.uniqueLabel || t.m4unique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(sys.xp)}`);
    return;
  }
  const pivNames = sys.pivotColsA.map((c) => `x_{${c + 1}}`).join(',\\,');
  const freeNames = sys.freeCols.map((c) => `x_{${c + 1}}`).join(',\\,');
  push(t.m4vars(pivNames, freeNames), readingTexSys(sys.R, sys.pivotColsA, sys.freeCols, n));
  push(t.m4part, `\\mathbf{x}_p \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(sys.xp)}`);
  push(t.m4hom(freeNames), basisTex(sys.kbasis));
}

function narrateNumeric(A, b, aug, field, n, t) {
  const blocks = [];
  const push = (html, tex) => blocks.push(tex !== undefined ? { html, tex } : { html });

  const sys = closeSystem(aug, field, n);
  const d = sys.nullity;

  // --- Momento 1: Rouché-Capelli
  push(`<h3>${t.m1}</h3>` + t.m1body, t.m1tex(sys.rankA, sys.rankAug));
  if (sys.incompatible) {
    push(t.m1incompat);
  } else {
    push(t.m1compat, t.m1dimTex(n, sys.rankA, d));
    push(d === 0 ? t.m1unique : t.m1inf(d));
  }

  // --- Momenti 2 e 3: scorciatoie e valutazione
  const { eyePart, colSc } = narrateShortcuts(A, b, field, d, sys.incompatible, t, push);

  // --- Momento 4: Gauss-Jordan sull'aumentata
  push(`<h3>${t.m4}</h3>` + t.m4intro);
  push(t.m4rref, `[A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}]_{\\text{RREF}} \\;=\\; ${augTex(sys.R, n)}`);
  if (sys.incompatible) {
    const badRow = sys.pivotCols.indexOf(n) + 1;
    push(t.m4badRow(badRow));
  } else {
    narrateSolutionFromRref(sys, n, t, push);
  }

  // --- Momento 5: forma parametrica e conclusione
  push(`<h3>${t.m5}</h3>`);
  if (sys.incompatible) {
    push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex);
    return blocks;
  }
  // confronti incrociati con le scorciatoie
  if (eyePart) {
    if (d === 0) {
      const same = eyePart.xp.every((e, i) => e.equals(sys.xp[i]));
      push(t.m5checkXpEq(same));
    } else {
      const diff = eyePart.xp.map((e, i) => e.sub(sys.xp[i]));
      const rk = rankOfVectors(sys.kbasis, field);
      const rku = rankOfVectors([...sys.kbasis, diff], field);
      push(t.m5checkXp(rk === rku));
    }
  }
  if (colSc.count > 0 && d > 0) {
    const ra = rankOfVectors(colSc.vectors, field);
    const rb = rankOfVectors(sys.kbasis, field);
    const ru = rankOfVectors([...colSc.vectors, ...sys.kbasis], field);
    push(t.m5checkKer, t.m5tex(ra, rb, ru));
    if (sameSpan(colSc.vectors, sys.kbasis, field)) push(t.m5same);
  }
  if (d === 0) {
    push(t.m5conclUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(sys.xp)}`);
  } else {
    push(t.m5structIntro);
    push(t.m5concl(d), paramFormTex(sys.xp, sys.kbasis, sys.freeCols));
  }
  return blocks;
}

function narrateParametric(A, b, aug, field, vars, n, t) {
  const blocks = [];
  const push = (html, tex) => blocks.push(tex !== undefined ? { html, tex } : { html });
  const a = analyzeParametricSystem(aug, field, n);
  const g = a.generic;
  const d = g.nullity;

  // --- Momento 1 (generico; se non ci sono assunzioni, vale per OGNI valore)
  const noAssume = g.assumptions.length === 0;
  push(`<h3>${t.m1}</h3>` + t.m1body, t.m1tex(g.rankA, g.rankAug));
  if (g.incompatible) push(noAssume ? t.pAllIncompat(g.rankA, g.rankAug) : t.pGenericIncompat(g.rankA, g.rankAug));
  else {
    push(noAssume ? t.pAllCompat(g.rankA, g.rankAug, d) : t.pGenericCompat(g.rankA, g.rankAug, d),
      t.m1dimTex(n, g.rankA, d));
  }

  // --- Momenti 2 e 3: scorciatoie (fatti COSTANTI, validi per ogni valore del parametro)
  const { eyePart, colSc } = narrateShortcuts(A, b, field, d, g.incompatible, t, push);

  // --- Momento 4: RREF generica (PRIMA delle condizioni, così si vede da dove escono)
  push(`<h3>${t.m4}</h3>` + t.m4intro);
  push(t.m4rref, `[A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}]_{\\text{RREF}} \\;=\\; ${augTex(g.R, n)}`);
  if (g.assumptions.length) push(t.pAssume(g.assumptions.map((x) => condTex(x.element.num))));
  else push(t.pNoAssume);
  if (g.incompatible) {
    const badRow = g.pivotCols.indexOf(n) + 1;
    push(t.m4badRow(badRow));
  } else {
    push(noAssume ? t.pAllSol : t.pGenericSol);
    narrateSolutionFromRref(g, n, t, push);
  }

  // --- Momento 5 (generico)
  push(`<h3>${t.m5}</h3>`);
  if (g.incompatible) {
    push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex);
  } else {
    if (eyePart) {
      if (d === 0) {
        const same = eyePart.xp.every((e, i) => e.equals(g.xp[i]));
        push(t.m5checkXpEq(same));
      } else {
        const diff = eyePart.xp.map((e, i) => e.sub(g.xp[i]));
        const rk = rankOfVectors(g.kbasis, field);
        const rku = rankOfVectors([...g.kbasis, diff], field);
        push(t.m5checkXp(rk === rku));
      }
    }
    if (colSc.count > 0 && d > 0) {
      const ra = rankOfVectors(colSc.vectors, field);
      const rb = rankOfVectors(g.kbasis, field);
      const ru = rankOfVectors([...colSc.vectors, ...g.kbasis], field);
      push(t.m5checkKer, t.m5tex(ra, rb, ru));
      if (sameSpan(colSc.vectors, g.kbasis, field)) push(t.m5same);
    }
    if (d === 0) push(t.m5conclUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(g.xp)}`);
    else {
      push(t.m5structIntro);
      push(t.m5concl(d), paramFormTex(g.xp, g.kbasis, g.freeCols));
    }
  }

  // --- Analisi per casi
  if (a.branches.length || a.declaredConditions.length) push(`<h3>${t.mCases}</h3>`);
  for (const br of a.branches) {
    const param = br.condition.param, val = br.condition.value.toLatex();
    const c = br.closed;
    // verdetto del ramo rispetto al generico
    if (g.incompatible && !c.incompatible) push(t.pCaseBecomesCompat(param, val, c.rankA, c.nullity));
    else if (!g.incompatible && c.incompatible) push(t.pCaseBecomesIncomp(param, val));
    else if (g.incompatible && c.incompatible) push(t.pCaseStillIncomp(param, val));
    else if (c.rankA < g.rankA) push(t.pCaseDrop(param, val, c.rankA, c.nullity));
    else push(t.pCaseNoChange(param, val, c.rankA, c.nullity));

    push(t.pCaseSubst(param, val),
      `[A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}] \\;=\\; ${augSubstTex(aug, n, br.condition.param, val)} \\;=\\; ${augTex(br.subAug, n)}`);
    push(t.pCaseRref, `[A\\,|\\,\\mathbf{b}]_{\\text{RREF}} \\;=\\; ${augTex(c.R, n)}`);

    if (c.incompatible) {
      const badRow = c.pivotCols.indexOf(n) + 1;
      push(t.m4badRow(badRow));
      push(t.m5conclIncomp, t.m5conclIncompTex);
    } else if (c.nullity === 0) {
      push(t.pCaseSolUnique, `\\mathbf{x} \\;=\\; ${colVecTex(c.xp)}`);
    } else {
      const pivNames = c.pivotColsA.map((x) => `x_{${x + 1}}`).join(',\\,');
      const freeNames = c.freeCols.map((x) => `x_{${x + 1}}`).join(',\\,');
      push(t.m4vars(pivNames, freeNames), readingTexSys(c.R, c.pivotColsA, c.freeCols, n));
      push(t.pCaseSolParam(c.nullity), paramFormTex(c.xp, c.kbasis, c.freeCols));
    }
    if (br.residualParams && br.residualParams.length) push(t.pRecursion(param, val, br.residualParams));
  }
  for (const dc of a.declaredConditions) {
    const tex = condTex(dc.poly) + ' = 0';
    if (dc.kind === 'nonrational') push(t.pNonRational(tex, dc.approx, dc.degree, dc.realCount));
    else push(t.pDeclared(tex));
  }
  return blocks;
}

// ====================================================================== WIRING
const __lsRoot = typeof document !== 'undefined' ? document.querySelector('.ls-root') : null;
if (__lsRoot) initLinearSystemSolver(__lsRoot);

function initLinearSystemSolver(root) {
  const LANG = (root.dataset.lang || document.documentElement.lang || 'it')
    .toLowerCase().startsWith('en') ? 'en' : 'it';
  const S = {
    it: { parseErr: (msg) => 'Cella non valida: ' + msg },
    en: { parseErr: (msg) => 'Invalid cell: ' + msg },
  }[LANG];

  const inRows = root.querySelector('#lsRows');
  const inCols = root.querySelector('#lsCols');
  const gridHost = root.querySelector('#lsGrid');
  const bHost = root.querySelector('#lsBGrid');
  const parenAL = root.querySelector('.ls-paren-al');
  const parenAR = root.querySelector('.ls-paren-ar');
  const parenBL = root.querySelector('.ls-paren-bl');
  const parenBR = root.querySelector('.ls-paren-br');
  const xeq = root.querySelector('#lsXeq');
  const statusEl = root.querySelector('#lsStatus');
  const out = root.querySelector('#lsOut');

  let rows = 3, cols = 3;

  const KOPTS = { throwOnError: false, displayMode: true };
  const RMOPTS = {
    throwOnError: false,
    delimiters: [
      { left: '\\(', right: '\\)', display: false },
      { left: '\\[', right: '\\]', display: true },
    ],
  };
  function renderKatexBlock(tex) {
    const safe = tex.replace(/\\mspace/g, '\\mkern');
    try { return katex.renderToString(safe, KOPTS); }
    catch (e) { return '<span style="color:#e55">[KaTeX error]</span>'; }
  }
  function renderInline(el) { try { renderMathInElement(el, RMOPTS); } catch (e) {} }
  function tip(msg) { statusEl.textContent = msg; statusEl.className = 'ls-status ls-error'; }
  function unTip() { statusEl.textContent = ''; statusEl.className = 'ls-status'; }
  function reveal(el) { el.hidden = false; el.style.display = 'block'; requestAnimationFrame(() => el.classList.add('ls-visible')); }

  function buildGrid() {
    rows = Math.max(1, Math.min(8, parseInt(inRows.value, 10) || 1));
    cols = Math.max(1, Math.min(8, parseInt(inCols.value, 10) || 1));
    let table = '<table class="ls-grid"><tbody>';
    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
      table += '<tr>';
      for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
        table += '<td><input class="ls-cell" type="text" inputmode="text" autocomplete="off" '
          + 'data-row="' + r + '" data-col="' + c + '" value="0"></td>';
      }
      table += '</tr>';
    }
    table += '</tbody></table>';
    gridHost.innerHTML = table;

    let btable = '<table class="ls-grid"><tbody>';
    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
      btable += '<tr><td><input class="ls-cell ls-cell-b" type="text" inputmode="text" autocomplete="off" '
        + 'data-brow="' + r + '" value="0"></td></tr>';
    }
    btable += '</tbody></table>';
    bHost.innerHTML = btable;

    renderXeq();
    fitBrackets();
  }

  function renderXeq() {
    const xv = [];
    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) xv.push('x_{' + (c + 1) + '}');
    const tex = '\\;\\cdot\\;\\begin{pmatrix}' + xv.join('\\\\') + '\\end{pmatrix}\\;=\\;';
    try { xeq.innerHTML = katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: false, displayMode: false }); }
    catch (e) { xeq.innerHTML = ''; }
  }

  // ---- Parentesi SVG: path matematico reale di KaTeX (tallDelim), come KernelSolver ----
  const PAREN_VBW = 875;
  const PAREN_CAP_HEIGHT = 3600;
  function lsLparenPath(midHeight) {
    return `M863,9c0,-2,-2,-5,-6,-9c0,0,-17,0,-17,0c-12.7,0,-19.3,0.3,-20,1
c-5.3,5.3,-10.3,11,-15,17c-242.7,294.7,-395.3,682,-458,1162c-21.3,163.3,-33.3,349,
-36,557 l0,${midHeight + 84}c0.2,6,0,26,0,60c2,159.3,10,310.7,24,454c53.3,528,210,
949.7,470,1265c4.7,6,9.7,11.7,15,17c0.7,0.7,7,1,19,1c0,0,18,0,18,0c4,-4,6,-7,6,-9
c0,-2.7,-3.3,-8.7,-10,-18c-135.3,-192.7,-235.5,-414.3,-300.5,-665c-65,-250.7,-102.5,
-544.7,-112.5,-882c-2,-104,-3,-167,-3,-189
l0,-${midHeight + 92}c0,-162.7,5.7,-314,17,-454c20.7,-272,63.7,-513,129,-723c65.3,
-210,155.3,-396.3,270,-559c6.7,-9.3,10,-15.3,10,-18z`;
  }
  function lsRparenPath(midHeight) {
    return `M76,0c-16.7,0,-25,3,-25,9c0,2,2,6.3,6,13c21.3,28.7,42.3,60.3,
63,95c96.7,156.7,172.8,332.5,228.5,527.5c55.7,195,92.8,416.5,111.5,664.5
c11.3,139.3,17,290.7,17,454c0,28,1.7,43,3.3,45l0,${midHeight + 9}
c-3,4,-3.3,16.7,-3.3,38c0,162,-5.7,313.7,-17,455c-18.7,248,-55.8,469.3,-111.5,664
c-55.7,194.7,-131.8,370.3,-228.5,527c-20.7,34.7,-41.7,66.3,-63,95c-2,3.3,-4,7,-6,11
c0,7.3,5.7,11,17,11c0,0,11,0,11,0c9.3,0,14.3,-0.3,15,-1c5.3,-5.3,10.3,-11,15,-17
c242.7,-294.7,395.3,-681.7,458,-1161c21.3,-164.7,33.3,-350.7,36,-558
l0,-${midHeight + 144}c-2,-159.3,-10,-310.7,-24,-454c-53.3,-528,-210,-949.7,
-470,-1265c-4.7,-6,-9.7,-11.7,-15,-17c-0.7,-0.7,-6.7,-1,-18,-1z`;
  }
  function lsParenSvg(side, heightPx, widthPx) {
    const k = widthPx / PAREN_VBW;
    let viewBoxHeight = Math.round(heightPx / k);
    let midHeight = viewBoxHeight - PAREN_CAP_HEIGHT;
    if (midHeight < 0) midHeight = 0;
    viewBoxHeight = PAREN_CAP_HEIGHT + midHeight;
    const renderedHeight = viewBoxHeight * k;
    const d = side === 'l' ? lsLparenPath(midHeight) : lsRparenPath(midHeight);
    const w = widthPx.toFixed(2), h = renderedHeight.toFixed(2);
    const style = `display:block !important;flex:0 0 auto;`
      + `width:${w}px !important;height:${h}px !important;`
      + `max-width:none !important;opacity:1 !important;visibility:visible !important`;
    return `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 ${PAREN_VBW} ${viewBoxHeight}" width="${w}" height="${h}" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="${style}" aria-hidden="true"><path d="${d}" fill="currentColor"/></svg>`;
  }

  function fitBrackets(tries) {
    tries = tries || 0;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      const gridA = gridHost.querySelector('.ls-grid');
      const gridB = bHost.querySelector('.ls-grid');
      if (!gridA || gridA.getBoundingClientRect().height <= 0
        || !gridB || gridB.getBoundingClientRect().height <= 0) {
        if (tries < 12) fitBrackets(tries + 1);
        return;
      }
      const fontSizePx = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(parenAL).fontSize) || 16;
      const widthPx = fontSizePx * 0.875;
      const hA = gridA.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      const hB = gridB.getBoundingClientRect().height;
      parenAL.innerHTML = lsParenSvg('l', hA, widthPx);
      parenAR.innerHTML = lsParenSvg('r', hA, widthPx);
      parenBL.innerHTML = lsParenSvg('l', hB, widthPx);
      parenBR.innerHTML = lsParenSvg('r', hB, widthPx);
    });
  }

  function gatherCells() {
    const aData = [];
    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
      const row = [];
      for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
        const inp = gridHost.querySelector('.ls-cell[data-row="' + r + '"][data-col="' + c + '"]');
        row.push(inp ? inp.value : '0');
      }
      aData.push(row);
    }
    const bData = [];
    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
      const inp = bHost.querySelector('.ls-cell[data-brow="' + r + '"]');
      bData.push(inp ? inp.value : '0');
    }
    return { aData, bData };
  }

  function clearOut() { out.innerHTML = ''; out.classList.remove('ls-visible'); out.hidden = true; out.style.display = 'none'; }

  function renderBlocks(blocks) {
    out.innerHTML = '';
    for (const bl of blocks) {
      if (bl.html) {
        const div = document.createElement('div');
        div.className = 'ls-text';
        div.innerHTML = bl.html.replace(/\\mspace/g, '\\mkern');
        out.appendChild(div);
        renderInline(div);
      }
      if (bl.tex !== undefined) {
        const wrap = document.createElement('div');
        wrap.className = 'ls-katex';
        wrap.innerHTML = renderKatexBlock(bl.tex);
        out.appendChild(wrap);
      }
    }
    reveal(out);
  }

  let lsRandomUsed = false;

  function lsTrack(mode) {
    const paramsUsed = [...root.querySelectorAll('.ls-cell')].some((el) => /[a-zA-Z]/.test(el.value));
    if (window.umami) umami.track('linearsystem-solve', { mode, rows, cols, randomUsed: lsRandomUsed, paramsUsed });
  }

  function step() {
    unTip();
    const { aData, bData } = gatherCells();
    let blocks;
    try { blocks = buildNarration(aData, bData, LANG); }
    catch (e) { tip(S.parseErr(e.message || String(e))); return; }
    renderBlocks(blocks);
    lsTrack('steps');
  }

  function quick() {
    unTip();
    const { aData, bData } = gatherCells();
    let blocks;
    try { blocks = buildQuickResult(aData, bData, LANG); }
    catch (e) { tip(S.parseErr(e.message || String(e))); return; }
    renderBlocks(blocks);
    lsTrack('quick');
  }

  function reset() {
    root.querySelectorAll('.ls-cell').forEach((inp) => { inp.value = '0'; });
    clearOut(); unTip();
    lsRandomUsed = false;
  }
  function randomize() {
    gridHost.querySelectorAll('.ls-cell').forEach((inp) => {
      inp.value = String(Math.floor(Math.random() * 7) - 3); // -3..3
    });
    bHost.querySelectorAll('.ls-cell').forEach((inp) => {
      inp.value = String(Math.floor(Math.random() * 9) - 4); // -4..4
    });
    clearOut(); unTip();
    lsRandomUsed = true;
  }

  // allargamento cella in focus (porting da KernelSolver / Gauss-Jordan)
  let blurTimer = null;
  function setFocus(cell) {
    root.querySelectorAll('.ls-cell').forEach((el) => el.classList.toggle('ls-cell-wide', el === cell));
  }
  function onFocusIn(e) {
    if (e.target.classList && e.target.classList.contains('ls-cell')) {
      if (blurTimer) { clearTimeout(blurTimer); blurTimer = null; }
      setFocus(e.target);
    }
  }
  function onFocusOut() {
    if (blurTimer) clearTimeout(blurTimer);
    blurTimer = setTimeout(() => { setFocus(null); blurTimer = null; }, 0);
  }
  gridHost.addEventListener('focusin', onFocusIn);
  gridHost.addEventListener('focusout', onFocusOut);
  bHost.addEventListener('focusin', onFocusIn);
  bHost.addEventListener('focusout', onFocusOut);

  inRows.addEventListener('input', () => { buildGrid(); clearOut(); });
  inCols.addEventListener('input', () => { buildGrid(); clearOut(); });
  root.querySelector('#lsBtnReset').addEventListener('click', reset);
  root.querySelector('#lsBtnRandom').addEventListener('click', randomize);
  root.querySelector('#lsBtnQuick').addEventListener('click', quick);
  root.querySelector('#lsBtnStep').addEventListener('click', step);

  buildGrid();
  renderInline(root);
  fitBrackets();
  window.addEventListener('resize', fitBrackets);
}

</script>